const lessons = {
  "5–9": [
    ["Artificial intelligence, or AI, is a way to make computers do jobs that usually need some human thinking. An AI tool might recognize a voice, suggest a song or help sort pictures. Not every computer program uses AI. A calculator follows rules that people wrote, while a music app may use AI to notice patterns in the songs you enjoy. People build these systems, choose what information they use and decide how they should behave. AI can be helpful, but it does not think or feel just like a person.","A music app may notice that you often listen to cheerful songs in the morning. It can use that pattern to suggest another song you might enjoy. A map app may also use AI to estimate which route could be quicker.","A map finds a quicker way to get somewhere. Could AI be helping?","It could be. Some maps use AI to find patterns in traffic and travel times. They also use maps, road rules and other information. A feature can use AI and ordinary software together.","AI (artificial intelligence): Computer technology that can do tasks such as finding patterns, recognizing things or making suggestions."],
    ["Machine learning is one way computers learn from examples instead of being given every single answer as a rule. First, people collect examples and show them to a computer. The computer looks for clues that appear again and again. These clues help it make a guess when it sees something new. People then test the guesses and look for mistakes. Learning from examples does not mean the computer learns exactly like a child or understands the world like you do.","Imagine showing a computer many pictures of cats and dogs. It might notice clues such as ears, faces and body shapes. After seeing enough different examples, it can try to sort a new picture. If all the cat pictures look the same, it may struggle with a cat that looks different.","If it only sees cats, can it learn what a dog looks like?","No. It needs examples of both cats and dogs. It also needs different kinds of cats and dogs so it does not learn just one small set of clues.","Machine learning: A way for a computer to find patterns in examples and use them to make a prediction or choice."],
    ["Training data is the collection of examples used to teach a computer model. The examples can be pictures, words, sounds or numbers. Each example gives the computer something to look for. Sometimes people also add a label, such as cat, dog or apple, to show the right answer. The examples need to be varied and useful. If important examples are missing, the model may not work well when it meets something new.","Imagine a plant-identifying app that has only seen pictures of bright green leaves. It may have trouble with a plant whose leaves are yellow, spotted or partly eaten by insects. Adding more kinds of plant pictures can help it learn that leaves do not all look the same.","What might happen if the computer does not see enough examples?","It may make mistakes when it sees something new. Good examples should show different colours, shapes, sizes and situations.","Training data: The examples a computer model uses to learn. A label is the name or answer attached to an example."],
    ["A pattern is something that repeats or something that different things have in common. You can find patterns in music, shapes, numbers and nature. AI systems look for patterns in the information they are given. For example, a system might notice that some pictures have similar shapes or colours. It can use those clues to group things or make a guess. But a pattern is not always a good reason, so people need to check what the system has learned.","Think about sorting animal pictures. You might group birds because they have feathers, or group animals by the number of legs they have. A computer can also compare picture details, but it may focus on the wrong clue if its examples are not varied.","Does a computer understand animals just like you do?","No. It can spot patterns in pictures, but it does not understand animals and their lives the way a person does. People decide whether the pattern is useful.","Pattern: A repeated clue or feature that appears across different examples."],
    ["A neural network is a computer model made from many connected mathematical parts. The parts are arranged in layers and pass information from one layer to the next. Each part works with numbers and sends a signal onwards. Together, the layers can help the model notice useful patterns. The name comes from the way the design was inspired by some ideas about brains, but a computer network is not a real brain. It does not have feelings, memories or thoughts like a person.","Imagine a team solving a picture puzzle. One person looks for shapes, another looks for colours and another combines the clues. A neural network uses connected mathematical steps to combine information in a different, much faster way.","Is a neural network a tiny human brain?","No. It is made with maths and computer code. The word network means that many parts are connected and work together.","Neural network: A computer model with connected parts that process information in layers."],
    ["Some AI tools can work with words. They are called language models because they learn patterns from lots of writing. They break text into small pieces and use the words around them to help choose what to write next. This lets them answer questions, explain ideas or make up stories. They do not look up the truth for every sentence they write. That means an answer can sound very sure but still contain a mistake.","You could ask an AI to write a funny story about a rabbit visiting the Moon. It can put words together to make a story that fits your request. But if you ask for a real fact about the Moon, you should check it with a trusted book, teacher or reliable website.","Does an AI always know if its answer is true?","No. It can make up things or get facts wrong. Check important answers with a trusted person or a reliable source.","Language model: An AI system trained to work with words and other pieces of language."],
    ["A prompt is the question or instruction you give an AI tool. It tells the tool what you want it to do. A short prompt can work, but it may leave many things unclear. You can add details about the topic, who the answer is for and what kind of answer you want. These extra details are called context. A clear prompt helps guide the answer, but it cannot make every answer correct.","“Tell me about space” does not say which part of space you want to learn about. “Tell me three facts about the Moon for a child” gives the AI a topic, an audience and an answer format. You can ask it to use simple words or give an example too.","Which question gives the AI clearer instructions?","The second one. It says what you want and how much to write. Clear instructions help, but you should still check the answer.","Prompt: The question or instruction you give an AI. Context: Extra information that helps explain what you mean."],
    ["Sometimes an AI can give a more useful answer if it looks at information first. Imagine asking a question about something you learned in class. Your notes may contain the facts needed to answer it. An AI system can search a collection of notes or documents and find parts that match the question. It can then use those parts when writing an answer. This idea is called retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG. The answer can still be wrong if the system finds the wrong notes or misunderstands them.","Imagine a class helper looking at your science notes before answering a question about planets. If it finds the page about the Moon, that page can help it answer. A page about volcanoes would not help much with that question.","Why might the notes help?","The notes give the AI useful information to use in its answer. You should still check that the notes match the question and that the answer says what the notes really mean.","RAG (retrieval-augmented generation): Finding useful information first and giving it to an AI model to help it answer."],
    ["An AI assistant can answer questions, explain ideas or help with a task. Some assistants can also use tools, such as a calendar, a search tool or a calculator. When an assistant plans several steps and uses tools to reach a goal, it may be acting like an AI agent. These tools can help, but they also make it important to have rules. An agent should only do things it has permission to do. People should check important actions before they happen.","Imagine asking an AI helper to find three library books about space. It might search a library list and show you the books. It should not borrow a book, spend money or send a message without asking first.","Should an AI helper be allowed to do anything it wants?","No. People set the rules and check important actions. An AI helper should ask before doing something that affects another person or changes something important.","AI agent: An AI system that can plan steps and use tools to work toward a goal. It still needs clear rules and permission."],
    ["AI can be useful, but it does not know everything and it can make mistakes. Sometimes it gives an answer that sounds real even when the answer is wrong. It may also miss important details or use information that is out of date. This can happen because the examples it learned from were incomplete or because it misunderstood the question. When something is important, do not rely on one AI answer. Check a trusted source and ask a trusted adult for help if you are unsure.","An AI tells you that a certain animal can live without water. That sounds surprising, so you check a nature book or ask your teacher. You discover that the answer left out an important detail. Checking helps you avoid passing on a mistake.","What should you do if an AI tells you something surprising?","Stop and check with a trusted adult or a trusted book. A confident answer is not always a correct answer.","Verification: Checking information against a trusted source before you believe or use it."]
  ],
  "10–15": [
    ["Artificial intelligence is a broad term for computer systems that perform tasks such as recognizing patterns, making predictions, understanding language or generating content. AI is not one single technology; different systems use different methods. Some products use machine-learning models, while others rely on hand-written rules or ordinary software. Many real products combine all three. It is useful to ask which part of a product uses AI instead of assuming the entire product is intelligent. People still design the system, choose its data and set limits on what it can do.","A music service may use a recommendation model to rank songs based on listening patterns, but its play button is ordinary software. A navigation app may combine traffic feeds, route calculations and prediction models.","A navigation app suggests a faster route. What evidence would help you decide whether AI is involved?","Look at how the route is selected. It may combine prediction models, optimization algorithms, traffic feeds and fixed rules; not every automated feature is AI.","Artificial intelligence (AI): Computer systems designed to perform tasks associated with abilities such as prediction, recognition, language or decision support."],
    ["Machine learning is a branch of AI in which a model learns patterns from data rather than relying only on rules written by a programmer. A model is the result of a learning process and can make predictions on new inputs. During training, an algorithm adjusts the model using examples and a chosen objective. The model should then be evaluated on data that was not used to train it. This helps reveal whether it learned patterns that generalize or simply memorized details from its training set. Machine learning still depends on human choices about data, objectives and evaluation.","A spam filter can learn from emails labelled as spam or not spam. After training, it is tested on a separate set of emails to see whether it can classify messages it has not seen before.","Why should we test with examples the model did not train on?","To check whether it learned patterns that generalize, rather than memorizing its training examples. A model that performs well only on its training data may fail in real use.","Machine learning: A method for fitting models to data so they can make predictions or decisions on new inputs. Generalization: Performing well on examples beyond the training set."],
    ["Training data shapes what a model can learn and what it may fail to recognize. Data quality includes accuracy, completeness, relevance and the way examples are labelled. Coverage describes how well the examples represent the situations and people the system will encounter. If some cases are missing or underrepresented, the model may perform unevenly. Labels can also contain human mistakes or assumptions. Good data work includes checking consent, privacy, representation and whether the dataset matches the intended use.","A face-recognition model trained mostly on images from one demographic may perform worse for groups that are underrepresented. The problem is not solved simply by collecting more data if the new data has the same gaps.","What is one risk of a dataset that does not represent the people who will use the system?","Performance can vary across groups, creating unequal error rates and potentially harmful decisions. Teams should measure performance across relevant groups and investigate gaps before deployment.","Training data: Examples used to fit a model. Representation: How well those examples reflect the people, objects or situations the model will encounter."],
    ["Machine learning includes several ways to learn from data. Supervised learning uses examples that include the correct label or target answer. Unsupervised learning looks for structure in examples without supplied labels, such as groups of similar items. Reinforcement learning uses feedback or rewards to help an agent learn which actions are useful over time. These approaches solve different kinds of problems and need different data and evaluation methods. A system can also combine more than one approach.","A labelled collection of photos marked “cat” and “dog” can train a supervised classifier. A system that groups music listeners by similar behaviour without being told the group names is closer to an unsupervised task.","Which approach needs labelled examples?","Supervised learning. The labels provide target answers during training. Unsupervised learning does not start with those supplied answer labels.","Supervised learning: Learning from labelled examples. Unsupervised learning: Finding structure in data without supplied labels. Reinforcement learning: Learning from feedback about actions."],
    ["A neural network is a model built from layers of connected mathematical operations. Each connection has a value called a weight, which affects how strongly information influences the next calculation. Layers transform input signals into intermediate representations and then into outputs. During training, the model adjusts weights and other parameters to reduce errors against a chosen objective. Networks with many layers are often called deep neural networks. Their behaviour can be powerful, but the many interacting parameters can make it difficult to explain exactly why a particular output was produced.","In an image model, early layers may respond to simple visual features such as edges or textures. Later layers can combine those signals into more complex features that help classify an object.","What changes when a network learns?","Its parameters, often called weights and biases, are adjusted during training. The network uses these values to transform input information and produce an output.","Neural network: A model made of connected mathematical operations arranged in layers. Weight: A learned value that influences how information passes through the model."],
    ["Large language models (LLMs) are neural networks trained to process and generate language. Text is split into tokens, which may be words, parts of words or punctuation. During generation, the model uses the tokens in its context to estimate which token should come next. Repeating this process produces a sequence of text that can look like a conversation, explanation or story. Training on large collections of text helps the model learn language patterns, but it does not guarantee that every statement is true. A language model can produce fluent text without checking the facts it describes.","A chatbot generates a response one token at a time, using the conversation so far as context. If you ask for a summary, it uses patterns learned during training to produce a shorter version of the information you supplied.","Does fluent writing prove that an LLM has verified facts or human-like understanding?","No. Fluency is not the same as factual accuracy or human understanding. Outputs can be plausible and still be wrong, so important claims need independent checking.","Large language model (LLM): A neural network trained to process and generate text. Token: A small unit of text the model reads or produces."],
    ["A prompt describes the task you want a model to perform. Context supplies relevant details, such as background information, examples, source material or constraints. A useful prompt often states the goal, audience, format and limits of the response. This can reduce ambiguity and make the output easier to evaluate. Prompt engineering is the practice of designing and refining these instructions. However, a well-written prompt does not add knowledge the model does not have and cannot guarantee factual accuracy.","Compare “summarize this” with “summarize the report for a project manager in five bullet points, separating facts from recommendations.” The second prompt explains the audience, output format and an important distinction to preserve.","What makes the second prompt more useful?","It defines the input, audience, format and expectations, reducing ambiguity. You can still review the result and ask for corrections when it misses something.","Prompt: The instruction given to a model. Context: Relevant information supplied alongside the prompt. Prompt engineering: Designing instructions to guide model behaviour."],
    ["Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) combines information retrieval with language generation. A system first searches a collection of documents for passages relevant to a question. It then supplies those passages as context to a language model, which uses them to draft an answer. This can make answers more relevant to a particular knowledge base and can help expose the evidence behind a response. RAG does not automatically make an answer correct: the search may retrieve the wrong passages, the documents may be outdated, or the model may misrepresent the evidence. Source quality and retrieval quality both matter.","A support assistant might search approved product documentation and pass matching passages to a language model. If the right version of the documentation is missing, the answer may still be incomplete even if the model writes clearly.","What should you check when a RAG answer is incorrect?","Check whether the right documents were indexed and retrieved, whether the passages were relevant and current, and whether the model represented them accurately.","RAG (retrieval-augmented generation): A method that retrieves relevant source information and supplies it to a language model during answer generation."],
    ["An AI assistant usually responds to a user's request, while an AI agent can also select actions and use tools to work toward a goal. An agent may keep track of its task, observe tool results and decide what to do next. This repeated cycle is sometimes called an agent loop. Tools might include search, code execution, calendars or business systems. More autonomy creates more risk, so permissions, logging, limits and human approval become important. The system should only receive the access it needs for its task.","A travel-planning agent might search flights, compare options and prepare an itinerary. It should request approval before purchasing a ticket or sharing personal information with another service.","How is an agent different from a chatbot that only replies?","An agent can take actions through tools and iterate toward a goal, rather than only generating a message. Its permissions and actions should be limited and observable.","AI agent: A system that uses a model, tools and a repeated decision cycle to pursue a goal. Human oversight: People review or approve actions when needed."],
    ["Reasoning-capable models may use additional computation or structured steps to work through complex tasks. This can improve performance on some problems, but it does not remove the possibility of errors. Models can misunderstand a question, rely on weak evidence or produce claims that sound convincing but are unsupported. These mistakes are sometimes called hallucinations. Verification means checking important claims against reliable sources, calculations or real-world tests. Safe use also means protecting private information and keeping people responsible for decisions that affect others.","An AI proposes a project plan. You check dates, assumptions, dependencies and source material before using it. If the plan includes a surprising fact, you trace it back to a reliable source rather than accepting the wording at face value.","Why is independent verification still important?","A confident explanation is not proof. Check evidence, test critical outputs and keep people accountable for consequential decisions.","Verification: Checking a claim against reliable evidence. Hallucination: An AI-generated claim that is false or unsupported but presented as if it were true."]
  ]
};

